WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I want to talk about the
campaign, as well,

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:04.037
because, of course, it's ongoing
despite the fact that, you know,

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of primaries have been
canceled, rescheduled.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:10.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've said you wanted to be
in the next debate.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:11.644 size:88% position:44% line:92%
How was the last debate?

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:13.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
I imagine it's
a little bit better

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
when there are
just two candidates.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:18.118 size:84% position:42%
How did you feel about
not having an audience?

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:20.387
-I think it's a positive,
to be honest with you.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.822 line:92%
I have not been a great fan of

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:26.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
the many debates
that we have had so far.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:30.563
I think when you're given
75 seconds or 45 seconds

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:35.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
to respond to a question,
often a complicated question

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's very, very difficult
to do that.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.373
I would -- You know, I kin
of think that what a good debate

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:44.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
is about is giving the
candidates the time they nee

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
to, you know,
go after each other,

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:50.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
discuss their differences
in an amount of time

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
that allows for
serious discussion.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.821 align:start size:94% position:6%
That has not taken place
up to now.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
The last debate, I think,
gave us a little bit more time

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:01.494
I would loosen up
the structure of it a little bit

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.463 line:92%
to allow for more interchange.

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I can say that I also enjoyed
not having an audience

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:10.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the last debate, but I do
think they're very vital

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:10.937 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
to comedy shows.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's been my experience
this last week.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.442
So I want to ask you this,
which is I think a questio

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:18.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of people in the
Democratic Party are asking.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you still see a path
to the nomination?

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:22.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, you're behind
in delegates.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.517 line:92%
It looks like a very hard path

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:28.021 size:88% position:44%
And, if not, why are you
remaining in the race?

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.890 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, we're about
300 delegates behind.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:35.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
Biden has 1,200. We have 900
There is a path.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:38.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is, admittedly,
a narrow path.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I would tell you, Seth
that there are a lot of people

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:42.669 size:81% position:41% line:92%
who are supporting me.

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have a strong
grassroots movement

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
who believe that
we have got to stay in

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
in order to continue the fight
to make the world know

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.714 line:92%
that we need Medicare for all,

00:01:54.714 --> 00:02:00.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we need to raise the
minimum wage to a living wage,

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we need paid
family and medical leave,

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the crises that
we're dealing with right now

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:09.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we must address
climate change and education -

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
all the issues that
we have been talking about

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:16.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
Campaigns are an important way
to maintain that fight

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
and raise public consciousness
on those issues.

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:23.543
So that's, I think, one of
the arguments for going forward.

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:26.379
-And do you feel as though
you've had a slight leg up

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the way you've had to
campaign in this digital age

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:33.453
with your early work as a
public-access performer,

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.054 line:92%
your days as a Burlington mayor?

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, we have put,
over the years

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:41.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a U.S. Senator
and as a candidate,

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:43.463 line:92%
a whole lot of effort into

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
social media
and into live streaming.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we have -- Given the fac
that I can't be out

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:52.272 size:84% position:42%
on the campaign trail,
the rallies, et cetera,

00:02:52.272 --> 00:02:53.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
we have been using
our live stream.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we usually get, you know
sometimes a million,

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:59.279 line:92%
sometimes 2 million viewers.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:03.683 size:84% position:42%
So we have put a lot of
resources into that.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.485 size:78% position:39% line:92%
We do it pretty well.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:07.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, I think we
do have an advantage

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:10.890 line:92%
over other folks in that regard.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:11.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Obviously, you're known
for having

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:14.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
an incredibly enthusiastic bas
of supporters.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:17.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're known for having
this incredible outreach -

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
not just in this campaign,
but the last.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is it safe to say that if,
for any reason,

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.737
you're not the nominee,
that you will try to root

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:29.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
that enthusiasm towards
Joe Biden?

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:33.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What I have said, Seth,
from day one, when I announced

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.885
that it is absolutely imperative
that we defeat Donald Trump,

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
who, in my view,
is the most dangerous presiden

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:42.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the modern history
of this country.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.926 size:78% position:39%
And we're seeing just
how dangerous he is

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:50.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all of the misinformation
that he is providing

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
during this
coronavirus pandemic.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, yes, we have got to
defeat Trump,

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:59.272
and if I am not the nominee,
I will do everything I can

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:02.875 size:78% position:39%
to see that Joe Biden
is elected president.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, thank you so much
for being here.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you for
making the time for us.

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:09.983
Stay safe in Burlington,
and hopefully the next tim

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'll see you,
it'll be in the studio.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay. You, too.
Take care. Thanks very much.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.989 line:92%
-Alright. All the best, Senator.

